<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             xmlns:vm="using:Patterns.App.ViewModels"
             x:Class="Patterns.App.Views.Sections.HelpSection"
             x:DataType="vm:MainViewModel">

  <StackPanel Classes="form" Margin="4,8,12,16">
    <TextBlock Classes="h1" Text="Help" />

    <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="KEYS" />
    <TextBlock TextWrapping="Wrap" LineHeight="22" xml:space="preserve">F1–F12   apply saved looks (Looks tab)
⇧F5   GO — open outputs
⇧F6   STOP — close outputs
⇧F7   IDENTIFY — flash screen numbers
⇧F8 / Space   BLACKOUT toggle
Page Down / Page Up   presenter click-through next / back (armed in Looks or Show)
On outputs:  Esc close · Space/B blackout · I identify · F1–F12 looks · PgDn/PgUp presenter</TextBlock>

    <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="BEFORE THE SHOW (PREP MODE)" />
    <TextBlock Classes="hint" TextWrapping="Wrap"
               Text="Outputs tab → PREP: build the whole rig at your desk with nothing plugged in. 'Plan a screen' adds a screen at the size the venue will have — arrange it, name it, give it its own pattern, put it in a canvas, use it in the multiview — and outputs stay held closed so nothing can go live by accident. Live inputs can be typed in by name (Media tab) before the camera or NDI sender exists. At the venue, switch to SHOW, pick which detected display each planned screen turned out to be and press Adopt: position, label, rotation, trims, warp, per-screen pattern, canvas name, multiview tiles and the stream source all follow onto the real display, so the desk work is never redone." />

    <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="MANY INPUTS AT ONCE" />
    <TextBlock Classes="hint" TextWrapping="Wrap"
               Text="Every live source runs on its own: give screen 1 a camera, screen 2 an NDI feed from the graphics PC and screen 3 a walk-in video, all at the same time. Pick the screen in the strip, then choose its source in the Media tab. The same feed used in several places (two screens, the PiP inset and a multiview tile) costs one decode, not four. Multiview tiles can name their own NDI source or capture device, so a monitor wall can show four different inputs. The Media tab lists what is mounted right now, and warns if the rig asks for more simultaneous sources than the limit (4 decoders, 6 NDI receivers)." />

    <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="WORKFLOW" />
    <TextBlock Classes="hint" TextWrapping="Wrap"
               Text="1) Arrange screens in Outputs — drag tiles together to span them into one canvas, click a tile to enable/disable it, rotate it for portrait mounting, trim its brightness/colour, nudge its corners (warp) for a keystoned projector, or give it its own pattern.  2) Choose and tune a pattern (or grab one from the Library).  3) GO.  4) Save the state as a look (Looks tab) and put it on an F-key, the daily schedule, or the presenter click-through order.  5) Run the evening from the Show tab.  Everything updates live while outputs run — including LED wall geometry, blend overlap, countdowns and branding." />

    <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="REMOTE CONTROL" />
    <TextBlock Classes="hint" TextWrapping="Wrap"
               Text="Switch it on in the Remote tab, then open the shown address on any phone or tablet on this network for the big-button web remote. Stream Decks: load the Bitfocus Companion module from integrations/companion-module-patterns (presets for transport, looks, screens, groups, presenter, stingers and audio, with live feedback) — or use Companion's Generic TCP with the one-line commands in docs/REMOTE.md. There is no password: anyone on the network can drive the show while remote control is on." />

    <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="THE SWITCHER (PROGRAM / PREVIEW)" />
    <TextBlock Classes="hint" TextWrapping="Wrap"
               Text="The right side works like a vision mixer. PROGRAM (top) is always what the audience sees. PREVIEW (bottom) is where you build — and it is sandboxed from the moment the app opens, so touching any editor never reaches the screens. The strip between them lists PGM, every joined canvas and every screen with its label: click a tile to edit it, use its switch to turn that output on/off live. Build the next look with any editor, then CUT (instant) or TAKE (crossfade) to every screen, or SEND TO TICKED tiles as their own pattern; EDIT SAFE re-arms itself after each send, so you are always building in safety. What you FIRE still goes straight to air: F-key looks, the ON AIR button on a look, scheduled cues, presenter steps, stingers and every remote command — only what you are editing is held back. Use → PVW on a look to load it into the preview instead. Blackout and GO/STOP stay live through the freeze. Turn the default off in Admin → Switcher if you'd rather the preview mirrored the program." />

    <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="SHOW PARTS, MULTIVIEW &amp; STREAMING" />
    <TextBlock Classes="hint" TextWrapping="Wrap"
               Text="Playlist parts (Media tab): split the playlist into named parts of the show; click ▶ to put one on air, give parts daily start times, or switch with SECTION n from the remote. Multiview (Pattern tab → type Multiview): a monitor wall of program/screens/inputs/clock with labels and red on-air tally — assign it to any screen or NDI sender, and watch it remotely at the web-remote address + /multiview. Stream tab: send one screen to up to two RTMP/SRT/UDP destinations with a single encode (full build, Windows); it never auto-starts, and stream keys live in your settings file, so treat saved shows as sensitive." />

    <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="STINGERS &amp; STAYING UP" />
    <TextBlock Classes="hint" TextWrapping="Wrap"
               Text="Stingers (Audio tab): one-press sounds and clips — a sound plays over everything while the music ducks, a video clip takes the screens and the previous content returns when it ends. Fire them from the Show page, the phone remote or a Stream Deck. Watchdog (Admin tab): the app runs supervised — after a crash or a 30-second UI freeze it restarts itself with the same settings and puts live outputs (and the audio track) back. A clean close never auto-restores. The health line on the Show page counts uptime, watchdog restarts and contained render faults." />

    <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="ADMIN (PERFORMANCE &amp; GPU)" />
    <TextBlock Classes="hint" TextWrapping="Wrap"
               Text="The Admin tab watches the machine while you watch the show: live CPU/memory/GPU/frame-rate with three-minute history charts, plain-language suggestions when something needs attention (on battery, memory climbing, frames dropping, disk filling), a computer overview with one-click support info, and a Restart app button that brings the show straight back. A rolling patterns.metrics.csv (30-second samples) records the night for after-show reading. Graphics card: Patterns detects and uses the best GPU by default and registers the choice with Windows (so video decode follows); pick Power saving, a specific adapter, or Let Windows decide — changes apply after a restart." />

    <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="PORTABLE FILES" />
    <TextBlock Classes="hint" TextWrapping="Wrap"
               Text="Settings, presets, brand kits and the log live beside Patterns.exe — copy the folder and everything travels with it. Show files (.patshow.json) capture the whole setup for a gig." />

    <TextBlock Classes="h2" Text="EXTRAS" />
    <TextBlock Classes="hint" TextWrapping="Wrap"
               Text="NDI: install the free NDI runtime (ndi.video) or drop Processing.NDI.Lib.x64.dll beside the exe — then send as many outputs as you need (8-bit BGRX or 10-bit P216) and receive any NDI feed as a media source. Video/audio and HDMI/SDI capture devices: the 'full' download bundles libVLC; the lean exe uses an installed 64-bit VLC or a 'libvlc' folder beside it. Web pages open on any screen via installed Edge or Chrome. All optional — the app runs without them." />
  </StackPanel>
</UserControl>
